  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Firestore in Native mode with 1.5 TB SSD storage</b> because it best satisfies the heavy read and write workload and the required capacity for a customer-facing web application.</p><p>This mode is designed for mobile and web apps and automatically scales to very high read and write rates while maintaining low latency. The target of 700 reads per second and 900 writes per second is well within typical scaling behavior as long as writes are distributed across many documents. Storage of 1.5 TB is supported since Firestore scales storage without manual provisioning and the SSD-backed performance characteristics align with the low latency needs of a high traffic system.</p><p><i>Firestore in Datastore mode with 1.5 TB SSD storage</i> is not the right choice for a new customer-facing app. Datastore mode is mainly for compatibility with older Datastore workloads and it is less suitable for real-time mobile and web patterns. It can also run into contention if many writes target the same entity group which makes it a weaker fit for sustained high write rates.</p><p><i>Firestore in Native mode with 1.5 TB HDD storage</i> is not valid because Firestore does not expose an HDD option and HDD characteristics would not meet the low latency expected by this workload.</p><p><i>Firestore in Datastore mode with 0.75 TB SSD and 0.75 TB HDD</i> is incorrect because Firestore does not let you mix storage types or provision storage in this manner, and Datastore mode is not recommended for new high traffic web applications.</p>",
